Implementation-steps:

1. Set up an S3 Bucket: This becomes your "Landing Zone."

2. Spin up AWS Transfer for SFTP: Point it to that bucket. Map your client SSH keys to the service.

3. Install Mountpoint for S3 on the Data Server:

  - Map the bucket to /mnt/client-data.

  - The Data Team won't even notice a change in their workflow; their files will just "appear" in that folder as clients upload them.

4. Lifecycle Policies: Set a rule to move files older than 90 days to S3 Glacier. This cuts storage costs by ~80% without deleting the data.


//  This policy allows the SFTP service to map the user to the bucket. Replace your-bucket-name with your actual bucket name.
// 
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Sid": "AllowListingFolder",
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": "s3:ListBucket",
            "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name"
        },
        {
            "Sid": "AllowUserSpecificActions",
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": [
                "s3:PutObject",
                "s3:GetObject",
                "s3:DeleteObject",
                "s3:GetObjectVersion"
            ],
            "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::your-bucket-name/home/${transfer:UserName}/*"
        }
    ]
}

// The IAM Role must also have a Trust Relationship that allows the AWS Transfer service to assume the role. If you don't add this, the user won't be able to log in.

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Service": "transfer.amazonaws.com"
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}